{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective","id":1112123,"title":"Query: Difference between V:tR and V:tM - A Player's Perspective","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-07-06T08:39:29-07:00","id":1112123,"title":"Query: Difference between V:tR and V:tM - A Player's Perspective","content":"Ok, so I have a few prefaces to my question.<br /> <br /> First is that I couldn't find a topic already on this, the closest being <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1028695-a-difference-between-chronicles-of-darkness-and-classic-world-of-darkness\">this</a> thread which was more about CofD vs WoD in general and focused on mechanics and setting. My question's a little different so I apologize if this topic has already been brought up.<br /> <br /> Second, I am posting this in the V:tR area rather than the V:tM area because I figured there is a higher chance of players having played both lines and players who prefer V:tR (and WofD in general) as I do.<br /> <br /> Third is the backstory to why I ask:<br /> My friend played V:tM way back in the day before there was anything else other than just V:tM so she has a lot of history with that game line and it's setting. When I started getting into CofD about 8 years ago she told me that she used to play V:tM back in the day and so this was the first she had heard of Requiem. After hearing me talk about it and my group and after a little research she decided that she didn't like V:tR and preferred V:tM due to some simplifications and some of the clans being taken out and other things. I suspect she prefers V:tM for the same reason I prefer V:tR: It was what we started with. (I have basically the same opinion of V:tM as she has to V:tR. I like the x/y system, the more focus on intrigue and webs of plans, and the mystery and and such)<br /> <br /> Well, her daughter is showing interest in V:tM so my friend might be running a V:tM game and has asked if I'm interested in joining. I'm not sure I like the idea of playing V:tM as I think I will see all the things I like about Requiem and not see them work like that in Masquerade. But I've never played Masquerade so I recognise that this is just a knee-jerk reaction. So I'd like to better know if I'd enjoy playing Masquerade.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> (tl:dr)<br /> <br /> <br /> So my question is this: From a players perspective: what is the difference between playing V:tR and V:tM?<br /> <br /> I know the setting of Requiem is more gothic horror and, under the skin, as it were where Masquerade's setting is almost Cyberpunk but without the cyber (I envision dirty streets and rough gangs of supernatural predators and gaudy Coven palaces on the edge of the city a la Underworld). I know the factions are pretty different and the clan differences are more pronounced in Masquerade.<br /> <br /> But what I'm looking for is what it's like to play in a V:tM game vs a V:tR game before I agree to play. To know if I'm going to enjoy myself or not.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://www.kickstarter.com/projects/200664283/demon-the-descent-prestige-edition\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://img513.imageshack.us/img513/4478/8vno.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> Frequent Story Teller for the Circle of Five gaming group.","edited":{"userid":654,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/654-xiongrey","user":"xiongrey","date":". 07-06-2017, 04:46 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/654-xiongrey","name":"xiongrey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar654_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"549"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-06T09:34:03-07:00","id":1112155,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>xiongrey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective#post1112123\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So my question is this: From a players perspective: what is the difference between playing V:tR and V:tM?</div> </div> </div> </div> Now, I don't know about the new Masquerade books, but there's a few notable differences I remember from pre-Gehenna era games.<br /> <br /> Clan is both your organization and your family. Yes, we technically have Camarilla / Anarch / Sabat / Tremere lines that sort of line up with Invictus / Carthian / Lance + CotC / Ordo Dracul lines, but those divisions are almost entirely clan based. As a whole, your allegence tends to be to the clan, not to any sect. Some clans are pretty bad, possibly offensive stereotypes as well, depending.<br /> <br /> Neonates have far less pull and sway. The Rulers That Be are powerful figures, and the way Generation works means that you won't really grow into powerful figures - there's no potential for it unless you start diablarizing. You can never Dominate your betters. You can never be stronger than them. They are eternal unless you somehow pull a coup and kill them through superior tactics and numbers. <br /> <br /> Its been over 15 years since my last game, and that's all I remember off the top of my head. A lot of it comes down to how well a GM downplays the bad and can run with the fun.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-06T10:52:21-07:00","id":1112207,"title":"","content":"I've played a lot of both, and from a player perspective I find the biggest difference is the setting. I've rarely felt that differently playing either version of Vampire, as long as the ST is good. I <i>like </i>the Requiem setting and systems better, but I've usually had more or less the same experience...I haven't played Masquerade in a while, though, so maybe I'd notice the difference now? It really does depend on the Storyteller. You can run Masquerade very intrigue-y and setting neutral, and you can run Requiem really punk rock and inclusive of all the lore.<br /> <br /> I <i>do </i>find that the Masquerade Disciplines are showing their age compared to VtR2E; choosing Disciplines in Requiem is much more exciting. The basic Disciplines in Masquerade feel like a step backward to me now. And VtM Celerity is a nightmare for any player who didn't take it. Multiple actions are terrible always and forever. I don't throw around the word broken, but it's broken. Apart from that, though, Masquerade Disciplines are a bit bland in comparison.<br /> <br /> I suppose the biggest difference, beyond the setting, is that (Celerity notwithstanding) VtM vampires feel a little weak now. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><b>Social justice vampire/freelancer | He/Him</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Actual Play:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/852858-vampire-the-requiem-%E2%80%93-bloodlines-ap\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire: The Requiem \u2013 Bloodlines</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Masquiem:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/5268-13-licks-the-curses-of-caine-in-blood-smoke\" target=\"_blank\">Curses of Caine in Requiem 2nd</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Storytellers Vault: </b></span><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=None%20More%20Dark%20Publishing#affiliate_id=321925\" target=\"_blank\">Author Page</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","name":"Yossarian","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-06T13:01:49-07:00","id":1112292,"title":"","content":"Every ST is going to put their own spin in each game, but the big difference, assuming your ST wishes to explore it? is that in V:tM your nights are counted. The world is about to end, and you can either dig your head in the ground like most or try to prevent the inexorable tide of blood... somehow. Or party like it's the end of the world and maybe hasten it all the way.<br /> <br /> How do you rally jaded immortals to that revelation after centuries of debauchery?<br /> <br /> YMMV, but to me there's little reason to play WoD unless you explore that there's an apocalypse (or 3 concurrent ones) about to happen.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-06T13:19:59-07:00","id":1112301,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1112292#post1112292\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Every ST is going to put their own spin in each game, but the big difference, assuming your ST wishes to explore it? is that in V:tM your nights are counted. The world is about to end, and you can either dig your head in the ground like most or try to prevent the inexorable tide of blood... somehow. Or party like it's the end of the world and maybe hasten it all the way.<br /> <br /> How do you rally jaded immortals to that revelation after centuries of debauchery?<br /> <br /> YMMV, but to me there's little reason to play WoD unless you explore that there's an apocalypse (or 3 concurrent ones) about to happen.</div> </div> </div> </div>IDK, if I were to run WoD again I'd indefinitely postpone the apocalypse. Gehenna's not imminent, the apocalypse is not imminent, and ascension is definitely not imminent. I like that the 20th anniversary editions downplay this aspect of the games.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","name":"Resplendent Fire","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2367_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1887"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-06T13:22:52-07:00","id":1112304,"title":"","content":"If the only draw for Masquerade is an Apocalypse for some people, did you end up just adding one to Requiem? Or playing up the existing ones?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 07-06-2017, 04:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T00:22:59-07:00","id":1112545,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Resplendent Fire</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1112301#post1112301\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> IDK, if I were to run WoD again I'd indefinitely postpone the apocalypse. Gehenna's not imminent, the apocalypse is not imminent, and ascension is definitely not imminent. I like that the 20th anniversary editions downplay this aspect of the games.</div> </div> </div> </div>Read the first paragraph of my previous post.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1112304#post1112304\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If the only draw for Masquerade is an Apocalypse for some people, did you end up just adding one to Requiem? Or playing up the existing ones?</div> </div> </div> </div>ChroD is built around personal horror, thus immersion. WoD is essentially about a need for escapism, with the big gothic-punk dramatic flair. It's about how societies collapse under their own weight, about inexorable entropy.<br /> <br /> In ChroD I can tell 10.000 different stories. In WoD it all goes by the way of the Metaplot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12191,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","user":"Malus","date":". 07-07-2017, 03:27 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T02:29:13-07:00","id":1112574,"title":"","content":"Well, I've run two games of Masquerade (but it's been a couple of years), and I've mostly been doing Requiem as of late.<br /> <br /> So, if memory serves Clan is far more important in Masquerade than in Requiem. Sure, you have the sects, but the only reason vampires are loyal to the sect is because their clan is in it, or more rarely they believe in its ideals.<br /> <br /> Disciplines also had some issues, but they made sense for masquerade: dominate not working on your elders, celerity being terribly good, and the blood sorceries being straight up cheating at higher levels.<br /> <br /> Onto the subject of Clans; there were 13 to choose from, 15 if you count the Cappadocians and Salubri. Then after clans you had many bloodlines to choose from.<br /> <br /> The rolling is a bit hard to get used to.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12436-ever-professional","name":"Ever Professional","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12436_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T06:37:10-07:00","id":1112641,"title":"","content":"I prefer Requiem because city politics are A LOT more interesting than Masquerade, plus you get access to Asian cities like Seoul, Tokyo, Beijing and Hong Kong, which were the sole domain of the Kuei-jin back then (which I loathed with passion)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12561-shawarbaaz","name":"Shawarbaaz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12561_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"467"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T09:15:08-07:00","id":1112725,"title":"","content":"Now, I've only played a few sessions of Masquerade, so this is largely an outsider perspective. But from where I sit, one of the key differences is in where the central conflict is directed. In Masquerade, the struggle is external. You get brought into a world where, even with your newfound supernatural abilities, you are completely powerless. The people above you are there not by their own merit but by birthright, and yet they have access to power you can literally never achieve on your own - the only way to get it is to steal it from them. It's a game about fighting back against a rigid system that seems built specifically to screw you over. Even the impending apocalypse is just another expression of that same metaphor - you're trapped in a doomed world, and the best you can hope to do when faced with the inevitable end is to fuck shit up for the assholes who let it get this bad in the first place.<br /> <br /> Requiem, conversely, is an internally-focused struggle. You are handed thr power to indulge your darkest desires, and then have to figure out what to do with it. The elders aren't the old blood that you can never hope to achieve, they're living examples of what you <i>can</i> and <i>will</i> become as the centuries pass and you gradually lose sight of who you were before.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T09:18:40-07:00","id":1112727,"title":"","content":"<br /> As a player there is a few differences between Requiem and Masquerade. <br /> <br /> The main one is that normally you know what you are getting into with Masquerade. There is sort of a &quot;default way&quot; to masquerade that you normally know that some common character concepts are gonna be viable in any masquerade game and DM will take the effort to let you know when they arent. For example: if i got a common character concept like &quot;snotty Ventrue&quot; or &quot;Creepy lurker nosferatu&quot; or &quot;punk anarch&quot; you know is gonna fly 99% of the time unless the DM is gonna change that, and he will let you know when he does if he would run &quot;in this game all ventrue got killed off in X date, or the anarchs here joined the sabbat, etc etc&quot;<br /> <br /> While in Requiem such &quot;default way&quot; doesnt exist. NWoD like to hammer in and on how you can make each city unique and not conform to the &quot;Standard&quot; but doesnt really give you an standard to rebel against. So a firebrand &quot;muh democracy&quot; Carthian might not viable if the DM decided that in his city all anarchs are fascist regime. <br /> <br /> With masquerade i have a common floor to think characters in for any game with any DM. A person tells me &quot;i am gonna run Masquerade&quot; and i already got in my mind a few character concept i can play, and when those character arent compatible is the exception rather than the rule. The same isnt equal in Requiem, in Requiem i have to see the city first to see what character concept i can begin to think for the game. <br /> <br /> In short the Chargen process (mechanics aside) goes:<br /> Masquerade: PC think broad strokes of a concept, DM comunicates any difference in the setting, player accomodates.<br /> <br /> Requiem: DM comunicates everything about it setting, player comes up with a concept that fit that. <br /> <br /> Mechanics aside both system arent very good. While OWoD is more simpler sometimes it also has some Chargen traps that have been mentioned. Discipline is a mix bag, Requiem have more mechanically tight disciplines but Masquerade has the fun ones like turning yourself into a Werewolf or control technology with blood magic. <br /> <br /> Theme wise, Requiem deal with occupying your time. The big question in Requiem is &quot;You are inmortal, now what?&quot; and all the covenants are answer to vampire who have way too much time on their hands now and need to do something. <br /> <br /> Masquerade on the other hand deals similarly but without acknowledging the issue. You can play a vampire who just does his own thing but you will be dragged into the Vampire society one way or another as everyone want you dead on their side. Its a very 80s feel, in which people feer the &quot;apocalypse&quot; that is mostly spout by crazy people and they just live their lives to the fullest.<br /> <br /> Aside from that another difference is the organizations. People tend to make the false equivalence of comparing both games Clans vs Clans and Covenants Vs Sect. Which is false, the correct comparison is Masquerade Clans vs Requiems Covenants as the groups you can &quot;choose&quot; to belong in the game and do politics with the others. Requiem Clans are politically meaningless unless the DM is making them a &quot;Thing&quot; and Sect is also meaningless because you dont choose your sect, your DM chooses it when he says &quot;i am gonna run a camarilla/sabbat game&quot; and unless he is comfortable with all the players switching side or is one of those weird games in which 2 of the 5 members of the coterie are Sabbat and the rest are camarilla the other faction is the enemy and thats that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1112292#post1112292\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">YMMV, but to me there's little reason to play WoD unless you explore that there's an apocalypse (or 3 concurrent ones) about to happen.</div> </div> </div> </div>I disagree, while i think that the splat\u00b4s apocalypse should inform the conflict and decisions of some of the characters in the setting/game. The apocalypse itself doesnt need to feature in the game. A good example would be the Walking dead telltale games (havent watched the series), it informs the character and conflict (being in the zombie apocalypse) but beyond that, the cause, development and conclusion of the event itself (the zombie apocalypse) is brushed aside in favor of the personal journey of the main character who are only trying to survive.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T10:15:23-07:00","id":1112770,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1112727#post1112727\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Masquerade has the fun ones like turning yourself into a Werewolf .</div> </div> </div> </div>Wait, what's that and how does it trump Requiem's protean?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3523-spencer-from-the-hills","name":"Spencer from The Hills","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"546"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T10:35:19-07:00","id":1112783,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spencer from The Hills</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1112770#post1112770\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Wait, what's that and how does it trump Requiem's protean?</div> </div> </div> </div><a href=\"http://whitewolf.wikia.com/wiki/Spiritus_(VTM\" target=\"_blank\">http://whitewolf.wikia.com/wiki/Spiritus_(VTM</a>) <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T10:44:36-07:00","id":1112787,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spencer from The Hills</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1112770#post1112770\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Wait, what's that and how does it trump Requiem's protean?</div> </div> </div> </div> I'll point out that a Vampire can gain the powers of a, Werewolf using the right scale of the Dragon, and that you can basically have a Wolfblooded Vampire that retains that ability to take on a werewolf form.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T11:18:54-07:00","id":1112801,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1112783#post1112783\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <a href=\"http://whitewolf.wikia.com/wiki/Spiritus_(VTM\" target=\"_blank\">http://whitewolf.wikia.com/wiki/Spiritus_(VTM</a>)</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Oh no, not the fancy speshul bloodline disciplines...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12561-shawarbaaz","name":"Shawarbaaz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12561_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"467"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T11:39:00-07:00","id":1112808,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1112545#post1112545\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">ChroD is built around personal horror, thus immersion. WoD is essentially about a need for escapism, with the big gothic-punk dramatic flair. It's about how societies collapse under their own weight, about inexorable entropy.<br /> <br /> In ChroD I can tell 10.000 different stories. In WoD it all goes by the way of the Metaplot.</div> </div> </div> </div>I've run many games in the WoD and I've never been forced to run according to the metaplot.<br /> <br /> And I don't think I agree that the WoD is more about a need for escapism. It's definitely got room for horror. Nor do I agree that the WoD is about how societies collapse under their own weight.<br /> <br /> Like, none of what you wrote resonates with me as someone who ran and played WoD games during their entire run.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","name":"Resplendent Fire","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2367_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1887"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T12:37:51-07:00","id":1112851,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Resplendent Fire</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1112808#post1112808\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I've run many games in the WoD and I've never been forced to run according to the metaplot.<br /> <br /> And I don't think I agree that the WoD is more about a need for escapism. It's definitely got room for horror. Nor do I agree that the WoD is about how societies collapse under their own weight.<br /> <br /> Like, none of what you wrote resonates with me as someone who ran and played WoD games during their entire run.</div> </div> </div> </div> So what would you say are the main differences between VtR and VtM?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T13:29:32-07:00","id":1112888,"title":"","content":"Alright so - as a person who started off as a Masquerade player and moved to Requiem, this is my perspective on things - it depends on your creativity as a person. Well, not exactly - it depends on your -need- for creativity, versus your need for structure.<br /> <br /> In my experience, there has never been a concept in Masquerade that I could not replicate in Requiem, or improve upon. The world is, in my opinion, far more dynamic in nature. This could be due to the people that I had the experience of playing with, but I've found my creativity a lot less strained trying to make a concept for a requiem character.<br /> <br /> That said, the benefits of Masquerade are structure. You can reasonably expect that a Ventrue in Masquerade is going to be lordly, and not a bloodline who has no interest in the throne because they are more interested in finding the holy grail due to some curse placed on them. This can make the games easier for an informed player and an informed ST.<br /> <br /> In the end, however - my personal experience is that I had a lot less fun with Masquerade due to the banality of the setting. It was kind of like playing in a fanfiction of someone's story, versus making my own story - which, as I understand, is a comparison you might hear quite often. I still -play- Masquerade and I still play requiem, but I play Masquerade more for the company I keep than the game itself.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1513-jakondite","name":"Jakondite","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"413"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T13:36:32-07:00","id":1112894,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1112851#post1112851\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> So what would you say are the main differences between VtR and VtM?</div> </div> </div> </div>VtR is smoother, has better mechanics, and lacks the metaplot. While you can play VtM without the metaplot, you don't have to work around it at all in VtR.<br /> <br /> VtM is a much more developed setting with global plots and named Kindred all over the place. This isn't necessarily better than VtR, but it is definitely a difference.<br /> <br /> Metaplot is of course a difference, but one I see as optional. If someone says they can't run Masquerade without relying on the metaplot, they're not trying hard enough. Heck, for first edition and a good part of second edition there <i>wasn't</i> a metaplot. There was only some plot in the form of adventures (such as Under a Blood Red Moon bridging Chicago by Night first and second edition).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2367,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","user":"Resplendent Fire","date":". 07-07-2017, 05:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","name":"Resplendent Fire","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2367_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1887"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T15:17:20-07:00","id":1112951,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1112727#post1112727\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I disagree, while i think that the splat\u00b4s apocalypse should inform the conflict and decisions of some of the characters in the setting/game. The apocalypse itself doesnt need to feature in the game. A good example would be the Walking dead telltale games (havent watched the series), it informs the character and conflict (being in the zombie apocalypse) but beyond that, the cause, development and conclusion of the event itself (the zombie apocalypse) is brushed aside in favor of the personal journey of the main character who are only trying to survive.</div> </div> </div> </div>A piece of setting that informs conflict and decisions within it is definitively featuring inside it. Even if it's just a catalyst for the story.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Resplendent Fire</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1112808#post1112808\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I've run many games in the WoD and I've never been forced to run according to the metaplot.<br /> <br /> And I don't think I agree that the WoD is more about a need for escapism. It's definitely got room for horror. Nor do I agree that the WoD is about how societies collapse under their own weight.<br /> <br /> Like, none of what you wrote resonates with me as someone who ran and played WoD games during their entire run.</div> </div> </div> </div>If you ran something featuring the week of nightmares, you conformed to the metaplot at one point for example. Same if you ran with a published NPC from a city supplement. Point is: that stuff was rigid, and it could cause a schism between players with expectations regarding it, for or against.<br /> <br /> The beauty of art is that it's all subjective. Again, YMMV.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12191,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","user":"Malus","date":". 07-07-2017, 06:23 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T15:28:59-07:00","id":1112960,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1112951#post1112951\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you ran something featuring the week of nightmares, you conformed to the metaplot at one point for example.<br /> <br /> The beauty of art is that it's all subjective. Again, YMMV.</div> </div> </div> </div>I know what constitutes the metaplot and I've been playing VtM since 1991. I don't need to be informed as to what constitutes metaplot. I never ran anything featuring the Week of Nightmares, the defection of the Gangrel, the Assamite faction that joined the Camarilla, the Harbingers of Skulls, the warrior Salubri joining the Sabbat, Gehenna, the destruction of the Tremere Antitribu (although that one is more plot than metaplot since it happens in Transylvania Chronicles IV). I did run the first two installments of Transylvania Chronicles, but again that's <i>plot</i> not <i>meta</i>plot. I <i>did</i> allow Camarilla Malkavians to take Dementation but that was a core book (VRev) change, and I did it before VRev was published at any rate.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","name":"Resplendent Fire","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2367_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1887"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T15:41:35-07:00","id":1112973,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Resplendent Fire</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1112894#post1112894\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> VtR is smoother, has better mechanics, and lacks the metaplot. While you can play VtM without the metaplot, you don't have to work around it at all in VtR.<br /> <br /> VtM is a much more developed setting with global plots and named Kindred all over the place. This isn't necessarily better than VtR, but it is definitely a difference.<br /> <br /> Metaplot is of course a difference, but one I see as optional. If someone says they can't run Masquerade without relying on the metaplot, they're not trying hard enough. Heck, for first edition and a good part of second edition there <i>wasn't</i> a metaplot. There was only some plot in the form of adventures (such as Under a Blood Red Moon bridging Chicago by Night first and second edition).</div> </div> </div> </div>Makes sense to me. The metaplot is not an essential feature of Masquerade. You can certainly ignore it and play Masquerade totally metaplot-free. It is, however, one of Masquerade's biggest selling points over Requiem, <i>if</i> you like metaplot. Just like the lack of assumed metaplot is one of Requiem's biggest selling points over Masquerade if you <i>don't</i> like Metaplot.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T15:49:54-07:00","id":1112974,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1112951#post1112951\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Same if you ran with a published NPC from a city supplement. Point is: that stuff was rigid, and it could cause a schism between players with expectations regarding it, for or against.</div> </div> </div> </div>Okay, nope. Using a published NPC from a city supplement is <i>not</i> metaplot. At <i>best</i> it is simply <i>plot</i>. By your definition as provided here, Vampire: the Requiem is loaded with metaplot in the New Orleans and Chicago sourcebooks, and in Chapter 6 of VtR itself. It is overly broad and as such not very useful.<br /> <br /> I mean I get the feeling you're trying to entrap me into saying that I used the metaplot, because I ran a few games set in Chicago because <i>Chicago by Night</i> which doesn't present you with a metaplot. It presents you with <i>situations</i> and characters involved in those situations, and sets it up so <i>player characters</i> can tip things one way or another.<br /> <br /> Most of my games were set in various cities in the Pacific Northwest and California, however, and were home brewed. The major exception being Transylvania Chronicles and Transylvania by Night, but even then it's not metaplot because the players are directly involved in many of the relevant events (such as the Convention of Thorns, the alleged diablerie of the Eldest, and the Anarch Revolt - and these things are <i>history</i> as they're first introduced as historical events that resulted in the modern Camarilla and Sabbat).<br /> <br /> But just using NPCs as written isn't metaplot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","name":"Resplendent Fire","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2367_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1887"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T16:50:54-07:00","id":1112993,"title":"","content":"Also, for what it's worth, I hate VtM's dice system with the fury of a thousand blazing suns,","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4854-justin-sane","name":"Justin Sane","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T19:45:48-07:00","id":1113063,"title":"","content":"either way, Masquerade isn't a -bad- system, per say. Its going to depend on how much you trust your friend to run the game.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T21:17:38-07:00","id":1113094,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1112951#post1112951\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A piece of setting that informs conflict and decisions within it is definitively featuring inside it. Even if it's just a catalyst for the story.</div> </div> </div> </div>By that wide definition then Dracula and Longinus feature on Requiem just because the Ordo Dracul and Lancea Sactum were funded respectively by them?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2179,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","user":"LokiRavenSpeak","date":". 07-08-2017, 12:56 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-08T06:32:10-07:00","id":1113218,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1113094#post1113094\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> By that wide definition then Dracula and Longinus feature on Requiem just because the Ordo Dracul and Lancea Sactum were funded respectively by them?</div> </div> </div> </div>Taxi driver from Bloodlines was a good execution, but I don't trust most STs to be able to pull it off in a satisfactory manner. Besides, ditch the specifics, I'm talking broadly. Broadly speaking, yes, Dracula and Longinus have an impact in the game, even if they aren't directly featured. Same thing with the overhanging apocalypse(s).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-08T06:36:22-07:00","id":1113220,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1113218#post1113218\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Taxi driver from Bloodlines was a good execution, but I don't trust most STs to be able to pull it off in a satisfactory manner. Besides, ditch the specifics, I'm talking broadly.</div> </div> </div> </div>What the taxi driver being Cain in bloodlines have anything to do with this? My point was that Gehenna doesnt have to freature in a Masquerade game as it only definew &quot;the sabbat believes in it, the camarilla dont&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-08T06:38:39-07:00","id":1113221,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Resplendent Fire</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1112974#post1112974\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Okay, nope. Using a published NPC from a city supplement is <i>not</i> metaplot. At <i>best</i> it is simply <i>plot</i>. By your definition as provided here, Vampire: the Requiem is loaded with metaplot in the New Orleans and Chicago sourcebooks, and in Chapter 6 of VtR itself. It is overly broad and as such not very useful.<br /> <br /> I mean I get the feeling you're trying to entrap me into saying that I used the metaplot, because I ran a few games set in Chicago because <i>Chicago by Night</i> which doesn't present you with a metaplot. It presents you with <i>situations</i> and characters involved in those situations, and sets it up so <i>player characters</i> can tip things one way or another.<br /> <br /> Most of my games were set in various cities in the Pacific Northwest and California, however, and were home brewed. The major exception being Transylvania Chronicles and Transylvania by Night, but even then it's not metaplot because the players are directly involved in many of the relevant events (such as the Convention of Thorns, the alleged diablerie of the Eldest, and the Anarch Revolt - and these things are <i>history</i> as they're first introduced as historical events that resulted in the modern Camarilla and Sabbat).<br /> <br /> But just using NPCs as written isn't metaplot.</div> </div> </div> </div>Read the by night supplements. Every scenario they presented was a railroad fest. I applaud you for not running with it at face value, like old school DMs would the Tomb of Horrors.<br /> <br /> A published NPC has a background, sure you can change that background but if I read the book and you tell me you're running X NPC I'll automatically assume that this background is in place.<br /> <br /> Did your players prevent the revolt for example? Or negotiate peace between the nascent Tremere and Tzimise? Doubt it. That's all encased in metaplot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12191,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","user":"Malus","date":". 07-08-2017, 09:53 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-08T06:40:01-07:00","id":1113222,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1113220#post1113220\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> What the taxi driver being Cain in bloodlines have anything to do with this? My point was that Gehenna doesnt have to freature in a Masquerade game as it only definew &quot;the sabbat believes in it, the camarilla dont&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>A piece of setting doesn't has to be the whole setting, but it will still color it. You're literally missing the forest for the tree. The fact that there's vampires making people into shock troops and tossing them to the lion's jaws after they have to claw their way out of their shallow graves because &quot;the end is near&quot; is already informing the setting, even if the red star won't collide with earth any time soon.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12191,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","user":"Malus","date":". 07-08-2017, 09:42 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-08T07:33:07-07:00","id":1113239,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1113222#post1113222\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> A piece of setting doesn't has to be the whole setting, but it will still color it. You're literally missing the forest for the tree. The fact that there's vampires making people into shock troops and tossing them to the lion's jaws after they have to claw their way out of their shallow graves because &quot;the end is near&quot; is already informing the setting, even if the red star won't collide with earth any time soon.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I would say that the Sabbat creating shovel heads is less about the gehenna and more about needing cheap disposable shocktroops to take on the camarilla<br /> <br /> But i see your point, i though you meant that gehena had to freature in every game meaning players had to engage with the end of times in every campaign to get the &quot;point&quot; of the game.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-08T11:11:21-07:00","id":1113294,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1113221#post1113221\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Read the by night supplements. Every scenario they presented was a railroad fest. I applaud you for not running with it at face value, like old school DMs would the Tomb of Horrors.</div> </div> </div> </div>I've <i>read</i> the by Night supplements. I've owned and read most Vampire supplements.<br /> <br /> The by Night supplements present you with a regional situation, but they exist for player characters to interact with.<br /> <br /> As far as having scenarios, not so much. Maybe later books? Of course there's Berlin by Night, with the silly-yet-not-metaplot story about Caine's alleged return. But not Chicago by Night, which is the only by Night book I ever ran anything from.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1113221#post1113221\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A published NPC has a background, sure you can change that background but if I read the book and you tell me you're running X NPC I'll automatically assume that this background is in place.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's not metaplot, though. Having a background isn't metaplot. If it <i>is</i>, then Requiem is loaded with massive amounts of metaplot (a statement I made in my earlier post that you ignored).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1113221#post1113221\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Did your players prevent the revolt for example? Or negotiate peace between the nascent Tremere and Tzimise? Doubt it. That's all encased in metaplot.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's not metaplot. It was history in Vampire: The Masquerade. Playing through the history is not metaplot.<br /> <br /> The thing is, my players could have dramatically altered the Anarch Revolt if they'd decided to get involved in that fashion. They might have changed the course of the Tremere vs. Tzimisce conflict if they'd decided to get involved. They <i>did</i> have an impact on events they involved themselves with.<br /> <br /> Unfortunately, your definition of metaplot is so broad as to be meaningless. History is metaplot, character backgrounds are metaplot. Basically <i>everything</i> is metaplot by your definition, and that just isn't the case.<br /> <br /> In any event, I'm not going to respond to you again, as all you're attempting to do at this point is force me to say that I made use of the metaplot (and constantly moving the goalposts on what metaplot <i>is</i>), and that's not a fruitful discussion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2367,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","user":"Resplendent Fire","date":". 07-08-2017, 04:35 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","name":"Resplendent Fire","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2367_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1887"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-08T13:55:41-07:00","id":1113362,"title":"","content":"Okay, I went to look:<br /> <br /> Chicago by Night, Chicago by Night 2e: Nope. There are two published adventures that are set in Chicago (Ashes to Ashes and Under a Blood Red Moon), but since they're adventures they're not metaplot, and they're not included in the Chicago by Night supplements. Chicago by Night 1e has a chapter devoted to random encounters, however.<br /> New York by Night: Nope<br /> New Orleans by Night: D'Jabbic. I remember nothing about this story, as I never ran it. Apparently there was something about a house having a basement/cellar in the adventure which is clearly pretty silly given that it's New Orleans, however.<br /> Berlin by Night: The Ascension of Caine, which is again not metaplot. I mean, it's a bad adventure, but the events are pretty contained to Berlin. I also didn't run this, although I did read it.<br /> Cairo by Night: Nope.<br /> DC by Night: Nope.<br /> LA by Night: Nope.<br /> Mexico City by Night: Nope.<br /> Milwaukee by Night: Psychomachia. I don't recall what this is as I never ran it.<br /> Montreal by Night: Nope.<br /> San Francisco by Night: Nope.<br /> <br /> 12 by Night sourcebooks, and three of them have included adventures. Sooooo... the implication that they were included in most by Night sourcebooks is not accurate. It probably is true that they're railroads, but that doesn't make them metaplot. No supplement relied on you having played those adventures, nor did any supplement refer back to those adventures. They were self-contained pieces. You could play them and move on, and you got to decide what form the fallout took after they were over.<br /> <br /> Can we not keep expanding what constitutes metaplot to the point of uselessness? Stick to what they are - overarching story arcs with mandated events that will turn out in a singular predetermined way whether player characters are involved or not, and either become the basis for future supplements or are at least referenced in future supplements. And while character writeups <i>can</i> be involved in metaplot (such as happened with multiple Children of the Night character writeups) they're not metaplot by default. Otherwise, Vampire: the Requiem is swamped with metaplot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2367,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","user":"Resplendent Fire","date":". 07-08-2017, 04:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","name":"Resplendent Fire","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2367_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1887"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-08T15:12:40-07:00","id":1113409,"title":"","content":"Vampire the Masquerade and the supplements were filled with metaplot. The first edition of Vampire the Requiem began incorporating metaplot extensively when they released Requiem for Rome. More than a few things in the second edition of Vampire is a retcon of some of the metaplot elements from first edition.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5466-tsusasi","name":"tsusasi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5466_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1028"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-08T15:20:50-07:00","id":1113413,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Resplendent Fire</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1113362#post1113362\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Can we not keep expanding what constitutes metaplot to the point of uselessness? Stick to what they are - overarching story arcs with mandated events that will turn out in a singular predetermined way whether player characters are involved or not, and either become the basis for future supplements or are at least referenced in future supplements. And while character writeups <i>can</i> be involved in metaplot (such as happened with multiple Children of the Night character writeups) they're not metaplot by default. Otherwise, Vampire: the Requiem is swamped with metaplot.</div> </div> </div> </div>You'll never see a Requiem supplement based virtually entirely on the outcome of a SAS.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1113239#post1113239\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> I would say that the Sabbat creating shovel heads is less about the gehenna and more about needing cheap disposable shocktroops to take on the camarilla<br /> <br /> But i see your point, i though you meant that gehena had to freature in every game meaning players had to engage with the end of times in every campaign to get the &quot;point&quot; of the game.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's what you took from it, and why I specifically told you to read my first post again.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12191,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","user":"Malus","date":". 07-08-2017, 06:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-08T15:27:28-07:00","id":1113416,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>tsusasi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1113409#post1113409\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Vampire the Masquerade and the supplements were filled with metaplot. The first edition of Vampire the Requiem began incorporating metaplot extensively when they released Requiem for Rome. More than a few things in the second edition of Vampire is a retcon of some of the metaplot elements from first edition.</div> </div> </div> </div>Requiem for Rome isn't metaplot, it's history. The thing about setting stuff in the past is that there are recorded events that occurred in that time whether real or fictional. The Camarilla was founded in Rome and fell with Rome. We knew this from Requiem first edition, if I recall correctly. It's not news, and it's not metaplot to cover those events in a supplement.<br /> <br /> And Vampire: The Masquerade wasn't filled with metaplot. V:tM revised had very little metaplot. Supplements <i>for Vrev</i> had the metaplot. First and second edition Vampire had very little metaplot at all - the very most or closest it came to metaplot was Under a Blood Red Moon informing the state of Chicago in Chicago by Night second edition.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1113413#post1113413\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You'll never see a Requiem supplement based virtually entirely on the outcome of a SAS.</div> </div> </div> </div>This has literally zero relevance to anything I said.<br /> <br /> Sorry, I said I wouldn't respond to you further, but this was so far out in left field I had to say something.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","name":"Resplendent Fire","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2367_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1887"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-08T20:31:00-07:00","id":1113515,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Resplendent Fire</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1113416#post1113416\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Requiem for Rome isn't metaplot, it's history. The thing about setting stuff in the past is that there are recorded events that occurred in that time whether real or fictional. The Camarilla was founded in Rome and fell with Rome. We knew this from Requiem first edition, if I recall correctly. It's not news, and it's not metaplot to cover those events in a supplement.<br /> <br /> And Vampire: The Masquerade wasn't filled with metaplot. V:tM revised had very little metaplot. Supplements <i>for Vrev</i> had the metaplot. First and second edition Vampire had very little metaplot at all - the very most or closest it came to metaplot was Under a Blood Red Moon informing the state of Chicago in Chicago by Night second edition.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> This has literally zero relevance to anything I said.<br /> <br /> Sorry, I said I wouldn't respond to you further, but this was so far out in left field I had to say something.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, there were a series of errands the PCs could run for the NPCs who were more important to the story in the books so PCs can brag they saw history unfold. But no matter what the PCs did the outcome was the same. That's metaplot. And turning the Macellarius bloodline from a Ventrue bloodline to a Julii bloodline is retcon. That is fine if you don't care about your character being unable to meaningfully influence the setting but it's metaplot, pure and simple and your personal dislike of the term doesn't change the fact that it's metaplot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5466-tsusasi","name":"tsusasi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5466_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1028"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-08T20:42:10-07:00","id":1113524,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>tsusasi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1112123-query-difference-between-v-tr-and-v-tm-a-player-s-perspective?p=1113515#post1113515\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> No, there were a series of errands the PCs could run for the NPCs who were more important to the story in the books so PCs can brag they saw history unfold. But no matter what the PCs did the outcome was the same. That's metaplot. And turning the Macellarius bloodline from a Ventrue bloodline to a Julii bloodline is retcon. That is fine if you don't care about your character being unable to meaningfully influence the setting but it's metaplot, pure and simple and your personal dislike of the term doesn't change the fact that it's metaplot.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't dislike the term at all and I do care &quot;about my character being unable to meaningfully influence the setting&quot;. But what you're describing is not metaplot. The outcome was known long before the books were published, because <i>history</i> is a thing that we were given before we were given any books that took you through that history. The story sounds <i>railroaded</i> and that's unfortunate, but railroaded doesn't necessarily mean metaplot.<br /> <br /> I don't know what relevance the Macellarius bloodline has to this discussion. Anyway, I doubt modern Macellarius Kindred are Julii at all, so this seems more like an elaboration on the bloodline's origin than an actual retcon.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","name":"Resplendent Fire","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2367_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1887"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-10T12:03:41-07:00","id":1114231,"title":"","content":"I think there's disagreement over the meaning of the term metaplot here?<br /> <br /> Metaplot is (in RPG terms) a living setting where the circumstances of the world change with each publication. Lucita joins the Sabbat, the Assamites break the Tremere curse, the Ravnos get blowed up real good; the official setting changes as time goes by. That book you bought where the Ravnos were the dominant clan of Desmoines, Iowa? No longer true. If you look at one book, and the given circumstances are distinguishable from those of a book published later on (as opposed to a new edition that changes the setting by fiat, rather than using the setting to justify that change), that's metaplot. Metaplot is what's to come. Setting is what came before. If any established event in the before of a setting can be defined as metaplot, metaplot isn't a useful term.<br /> <br /> I wouldn't call Fall of the Camarilla metaplot. It's an established piece of the setting (VtR1e mentions the fall of the Camarilla) that the players can interact with and shape; there's no canonical ending beyond the Camarilla falling. The adventure might be railroady to some, but it's not progressing a plot that's going to be continued in a new book -- there's no <b>City of the Damned: Constantinople</b> catching us up on Corbulo's true fate. To be fair, there are plenty of instances in Requiem where we get little updates on certain characters and events (Felix is a good example), but those rarely bear any weight on the setting as a whole. Like, to compare the closest equivalent, <b>Dark Ages: Vampire </b>has a metaplot. There are lots of plot threads that run through that line.<br /> <br /> All that being said, there's a lot of wiggle room. I just would argue that an adventure with a predetermined ending (however unsatisfying that might be) isn't metaplot.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><b>Social justice vampire/freelancer | He/Him</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Actual Play:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/852858-vampire-the-requiem-%E2%80%93-bloodlines-ap\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire: The Requiem \u2013 Bloodlines</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Masquiem:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/5268-13-licks-the-curses-of-caine-in-blood-smoke\" target=\"_blank\">Curses of Caine in Requiem 2nd</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Storytellers Vault: </b></span><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=None%20More%20Dark%20Publishing#affiliate_id=321925\" target=\"_blank\">Author Page</a>","edited":{"userid":192,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","user":"Yossarian","date":". 07-10-2017, 07:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":9}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","name":"Yossarian","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2297"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}